WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.174
Picture a corner shop with an odd habit.

2
00:00:05.174 --> 00:00:09.008
Every evening, the shopkeeper packs the whole shop into boxes.

3
00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:18.037
Every morning, she unlocks the door, switches on the lights, unpacks each box, checks every item against its delivery note, and fills the shelves.

4
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.772
Only then can she serve the first customer.

5
00:00:20.772 --> 00:00:24.851
And even then she's slow, because she still looks up where the tea is.

6
00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.856
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:28.856 --> 00:00:31.207
My main method is three lines long.

8
00:00:31.207 --> 00:00:36.714
Why does the JVM, the program that runs my Java and Kotlin, take so long to start?

9
00:00:36.714 --> 00:00:39.937
And doesn't it remember anything from last time?

10
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:45.733
Our running example is a small parcel tracking service.

11
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:50.176
One run on JDK 25 printed: started in 0.151 seconds.

12
00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:56.243
Yet the first answer reached the outside 429 milliseconds after launch.

13
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:57.498
Both are true.

14
00:00:57.498 --> 00:00:59.488
They time different things.

15
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.052
Here's a tiny demo with exactly one class of its own.

16
00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:09.151
It reads three lines of settings, formats a date, and joins a few strings.

17
00:01:09.151 --> 00:01:12.252
How many classes does the JVM load to run it?

18
00:01:12.252 --> 00:01:13.355
Pause and guess.

19
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:21.029
On JDK 25, it loaded 818 classes, one square each.

20
00:01:21.029 --> 00:01:25.257
Exactly one came from the demo: the green one.

21
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:29.012
793 came ready made, from the JDK's archive.

22
00:01:29.012 --> 00:01:33.203
19 were unpacked on the spot, from its modules file.

23
00:01:33.203 --> 00:01:37.071
And 5 were made in memory, for lambdas and such.

24
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.320
So we asked the shop for one box, and it unpacked 818.

25
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.331
Ours is in there somewhere.

26
00:01:43.331 --> 00:01:45.192
Bring a magnifying glass.

27
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.509
Jokes aside, most of the work is the JDK, not your code.

28
00:01:50.509 --> 00:02:01.093
Even an app with 300 classes of its own loaded 1,537 on JDK 21, with the archive off, and its own were only a fifth.

29
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.638
When you type java, a small native program, the launcher, starts first.

30
00:02:06.638 --> 00:02:10.362
Native means compiled for your machine, like a C program.

31
00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:14.478
It loads the JVM and asks it to create a VM, a virtual machine.

32
00:02:14.478 --> 00:02:18.005
That's unlocking the door and switching on the lights.

33
00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:22.409
The JVM reserves the heap, the memory where your objects will live.

34
00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:28.331
It builds its interpreter, starts its helper threads, and sets up the core classes and modules.

35
00:02:28.331 --> 00:02:31.011
Only then does it look for your main class.

36
00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:32.326
Then the boxes.

37
00:02:32.326 --> 00:02:41.652
The first time a class is needed, the JVM finds its class file and parses it into metaspace, the memory it keeps for class data.

38
00:02:41.652 --> 00:02:43.328
That's unpacking a box.

39
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:51.438
Next it verifies the bytecode, the instructions in the class file, checking that each one is type safe before any of them runs.

40
00:02:51.438 --> 00:02:54.363
That's the delivery note, checked item by item.

41
00:02:54.567 --> 00:03:01.576
Then your code runs in the interpreter, one instruction at a time, like looking up every price in the catalogue.

42
00:03:01.576 --> 00:03:09.399
The JIT compilers turn hot methods into machine code, but only after gathering profiles: counts of what the code really does.

43
00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:13.467
That's learning the popular items by heart, and it takes a while.

44
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:24.523
A framework, a library that starts and wires your program, adds chores of its own: scanning for annotations, creating objects by name, and reading configuration.

45
00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:26.736
Now the second shy question.

46
00:03:26.736 --> 00:03:29.025
Stop the JVM and start it again.

47
00:03:29.025 --> 00:03:31.743
How much of this morning does it skip?

48
00:03:31.743 --> 00:03:32.888
Pause and guess.

49
00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:36.266
Almost none.

50
00:03:36.266 --> 00:03:38.731
Nothing of this survives the process.

51
00:03:38.731 --> 00:03:42.463
The next launch unpacks, checks and learns it all again.

52
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.009
You have to admire the dedication.

53
00:03:45.009 --> 00:03:50.726
The same tins of tea, checked against the same delivery note, every single morning.

54
00:03:50.726 --> 00:03:51.690
Thorough, yes.

55
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:52.379
Quick, no.

56
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.637
So the point really is this.

57
00:03:54.637 --> 00:03:58.203
Start-up is work the JVM repeats on every launch.

58
00:03:58.433 --> 00:04:00.150
There's one exception.

59
00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:10.605
The JDK ships an archive of about 1,300 of its own classes, already parsed, and the JVM maps them from disk instead of unpacking them.

60
00:04:10.605 --> 00:04:13.102
Those were the 793 blue squares.

61
00:04:13.102 --> 00:04:16.145
The next lesson builds bigger archives.

62
00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:18.832
You can watch this yourself.

63
00:04:18.832 --> 00:04:24.696
-Xlog:class+load prints one line per class, and the end of each line says where it came from.

64
00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.429
shared objects file: ready made, from the archive.

65
00:04:28.429 --> 00:04:31.252
jrt: the JDK's modules file, parsed now.

66
00:04:31.252 --> 00:04:36.122
file: your class path, the folders and JAR files that hold your code.

67
00:04:36.122 --> 00:04:42.121
And a class name marks a hidden class, made in memory, like the one each lambda gets.

68
00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:46.390
Here the shop picture breaks, and the break matters.

69
00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:49.683
Our shopkeeper unpacks everything before she opens.

70
00:04:49.683 --> 00:04:55.235
The JVM opens first, and unpacks a box only when someone asks for something inside it.

71
00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:57.627
That's called lazy loading.

72
00:04:57.627 --> 00:05:02.669
It's why a one class demo loads 818 classes, not the whole JDK.

73
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.106
Three rules of thumb.

74
00:05:05.106 --> 00:05:09.838
The printed line isn't the whole story: the wait starts at launch.

75
00:05:09.838 --> 00:05:13.351
Count before you cut: most classes are the JDK's.

76
00:05:13.351 --> 00:05:18.944
And the archive covers only the JDK's classes: yours, and the JIT, start cold.

77
00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:22.808
So, why does a three line main start slowly?

78
00:05:22.808 --> 00:05:35.578
Because every launch runs the whole morning routine: open the shop, unpack and check each box when it's first needed, look up prices until the JIT learns, and let the framework do its chores.

79
00:05:35.578 --> 00:05:40.793
And the JVM remembers almost nothing, apart from the JDK's ready made shelves.

80
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:41.789
Quick check.

81
00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:43.957
A hidden class has no class file.

82
00:05:43.957 --> 00:05:46.652
Why can its source never start with file?

83
00:05:49.233 --> 00:05:54.936
Because file names where a class's bytes were read from, and a hidden class's bytes were made in memory.
